-- title:
--   Regular differentials of the modular function field have dimension the genus
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of finite index containing the translation matrix $T$. Let $F =$ [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC K Γ`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101) be the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $K((q))$ over $K$ generated by the set of all quotients $\mathrm{int}_K(p_f)/\mathrm{int}_K(p_g)$, where $f,g$ are modular forms of one and the same weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$ for $\Gamma$ (regarded inside $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$), $p_f,p_g$ are integral power series realising their $q$-expansions, and the base change of $p_g$ to $K$ is nonzero. Inside the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ consider the $K$-submodule [`AlgebraicCurve.regularDifferentials K F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_RegularDifferentials.html#L26) of those $\omega$ such that for every place $v$ of $F$ over $K$ — that is, every valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ and a principal ideal ring — there is an element $f$ of that valuation subring with $\omega = f \cdot \mathrm{d}\pi_v$, $\pi_v$ a uniformiser of $v$. The assertion is that this submodule is a finite $K$-module and that its $K$-dimension equals [`AlgebraicCurve.genusFF K F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Repartitions.html#L145), the genus defined as the $K$-dimension of the repartition cohomology $H^1$ of the zero divisor.
--
--   This is the classical identification, for a one-variable function field over an algebraically closed field, of the space of everywhere-regular differentials with a space of dimension the genus, here applied to the $q$-expansion function field of the modular curve $X(\Gamma)$. It feeds the computation of dimensions of spaces of differentials on $X(\Gamma)$, being used in [`ModularCurve.finiteDimensional_and_finrank_twoCompRegularDifferentials_add_one`](thm.html#ModularCurve.finiteDimensional_and_finrank_twoCompRegularDifferentials_add_one).
